<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William III of the Netherlands</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William III was King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and, until 1866, Duke of Limburg in the German Confederation, reigning from 1849 to 1890. He reluctantly accepted the liberal constitution of 1848 and the limits on royal power it brought. His sons died before him, and his daughter Wilhelmina succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="William III of the Netherlands" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maria Elisabeth Hille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_koning_Willem_III_door_Maria_Hille_HGA001008802_(uitsnede).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 19 February 1817 in the Palace of the Nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, the eldest son of the future King William II and Anna Pavlovna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He and his brothers were raised by military men and expected to join the army, and he studied for a time at Leiden University without taking a degree. On 18 June 1839 he married his cousin Sophie of Württemberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. The marriage was unhappy, and she bore him three sons, William, Maurice and Alexander.</p><p>William disliked the constitutional changes of 1848 introduced by his father and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke">Johan Rudolf Thorbecke</a>, seeing them as useless limits on royal power, and he would have preferred to govern as an enlightened despot like his grandfather William I. His father died on 17 March 1849, when William was a guest at Raby Castle, and he was reluctant to return to take the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His first act as king was to install the cabinet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke">Thorbecke</a>, designer of the liberal constitution of 1848, which had stripped the crown of most of its political powers. When the Catholic hierarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> was restored in 1853, he found growing conservative support and a reason to dismiss Thorbecke. In the first two decades of his reign he dismissed several cabinets and dissolved parliament several times. In the Luxembourg Coup of 1856 he unilaterally imposed a reactionary constitution on Luxembourg. In 1867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> offered to buy Luxembourg, which almost brought war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and France until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867">Second Treaty of London</a> confirmed Luxembourg as independent.</p><p>He grew unpopular with his bourgeois-liberal subjects but stayed popular with the common man. Queen Sophie died in 1877, and in 1879 William married Emma of Waldeck and Pyrmont, who was 41 years younger. Their daughter Wilhelmina was born in 1880 and became heir presumptive in 1884, after the last of William's sons from his first marriage died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William fell seriously ill with a kidney ailment in 1887 and was increasingly demented in 1889 and 1890, when the Council of State and then Queen Emma became regents. He died at Het Loo Palace in 1890, and Emma served as regent for Wilhelmina until 1898. Having reigned for 41 years, he is the second-longest reigning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> monarch, surpassed only by Wilhelmina.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William III of the Netherlands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1867 Napoleon III of France negotiated to buy the grand duchy from <b>William III of the Netherlands</b>, provoking the Luxembourg Crisis with Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>William III</b> (1849–1890) disliked these limits but could not reverse them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palace of the Nation, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Het Loo Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.37&amp;year=1864" aria-label="Palace of the Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4649 400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William III of the Netherlands"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5099 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.37&amp;year=1864">What was here in 1864? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
